    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 5 (Questions 25&ndash;30)</div>\n<p>Aerobic organisms utilize an integrated network of antioxidant metalloenzymes to neutralize reactive oxygen species (ROS) produced during mitochondrial respiration. The front-line defense against superoxide radical anions ($\\text{O}_2^{\\bullet-}$) is catalyzed by superoxide dismutase (SOD), which dismutates superoxide into hydrogen peroxide ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$) and molecular oxygen ($\\text{O}_2$):</p>\n<p>$$2\\,\\text{O}_2^{\\bullet-} + 2\\,\\text{H}^+ \\xrightarrow{\\text{SOD}} \\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2 + \\text{O}_2$$</p>\n<p>Mammalian tissues express three compartmentalized SOD isoforms. SOD1 (Cu/Zn-SOD) is a homodimer localized primarily in the cytosol and the mitochondrial intermembrane space (IMS), where catalytic copper ($\\text{Cu}^{2+} \\leftrightarrow \\text{Cu}^+$) executes redox cycling while zinc ($\\text{Zn}^{2+}$) maintains structural stability. SOD2 (Mn-SOD) is a homotetramer localized exclusively in the mitochondrial matrix, coordinating manganese ($\\text{Mn}^{3+} \\leftrightarrow \\text{Mn}^{2+}$) to protect matrix metabolic enzymes and mitochondrial DNA. SOD3 (EC-SOD) is a secretory copper/zinc homotetramer tethered to extracellular matrix proteoglycans.</p>\n<p>Because $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$ readily crosses biological membranes and reacts with unchelated ferrous iron ($\\text{Fe}^{2+}$) via the Fenton reaction ($\\text{Fe}^{2+} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2 \\rightarrow \\text{Fe}^{3+} + \\text{OH}^\\bullet + \\text{OH}^-$) to generate the hyper-reactive hydroxyl radical ($\\text{OH}^\\bullet$), cells maintain dual $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$ clearance pathways. Peroxisomal catalase dismutates $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$ into water and oxygen without requiring an auxiliary reducing co-substrate. In the cytosol and mitochondria, glutathione peroxidase (GPx, a selenoprotein) reduces $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$ to water using reduced glutathione (GSH), which is regenerated by glutathione reductase (GR) consuming NADPH.</p>\n<p>Researchers examined CRISPR-Cas9 knockout HEK293 lines challenged with paraquat (which generates mitochondrial superoxide) or exogenous $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$. Mitochondrial aconitase activity, malondialdehyde (MDA) lipid peroxidation, protein carbonyls, and cell viability were quantified (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Oxidative stress markers and cell viability across antioxidant-deficient cell lines</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Genotype</th><th>Challenge / Treatment</th><th>Aconitase Activity (%)</th><th>MDA (nmol/mg)</th><th>Protein Carbonyls (nmol/mg)</th><th>Cell Viability (%)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>WT</td><td>Vehicle (Control)</td><td>100 &plusmn; 4</td><td>0.8 &plusmn; 0.1</td><td>1.2 &plusmn; 0.1</td><td>98 &plusmn; 2</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>WT</td><td>Paraquat (1 mM)</td><td>82 &plusmn; 5</td><td>1.9 &plusmn; 0.2</td><td>2.5 &plusmn; 0.2</td><td>88 &plusmn; 4</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>$\\text{SOD1}^{-/-}$</td><td>Paraquat (1 mM)</td><td>78 &plusmn; 6</td><td>4.6 &plusmn; 0.4</td><td>5.8 &plusmn; 0.3</td><td>62 &plusmn; 5</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>$\\text{SOD2}^{-/-}$</td><td>Paraquat (1 mM)</td><td>14 &plusmn; 2</td><td>5.2 &plusmn; 0.5</td><td>6.5 &plusmn; 0.4</td><td>28 &plusmn; 3</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>shCAT</td><td>$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$ ($200\\,\\mu\\text{M}$)</td><td>80 &plusmn; 5</td><td>4.8 &plusmn; 0.4</td><td>5.2 &plusmn; 0.3</td><td>55 &plusmn; 4</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>WT + DFO</td><td>Paraquat + Deferoxamine</td><td>81 &plusmn; 4</td><td>1.0 &plusmn; 0.1</td><td>1.4 &plusmn; 0.1</td><td>94 &plusmn; 3</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
